window.ORGANIZER_LIBRARY = {
 "files": {
  "Job notes/brightwell_search/prep.md": "# Brightwell Labs: Senior Software Engineer, Search\n\n## Your opener\n\n1. Backend engineer who likes data-heavy features.\n2. Built the autocomplete service for your current product.\n\n> I've built search features before and I enjoy the mix of data and latency work. Search is the heart of Brightwell's product, which is why this role stood out.\n\n## Why Brightwell\n\n- Search is the core of their product, not a side feature.\n- Dev Okafor can refer you: ask before applying.\n\n## Questions to ask them\n\n1. How do you measure search quality today?\n2. How does the search team work with the data science group?\n",
  "Job notes/harborlight/prep.md": "# Harborlight Bank: Senior Backend Engineer, Mobile Banking\n\n## Your opener\n\n1. Backend engineer who builds APIs that mobile apps depend on.\n2. Cut checkout API p99 latency from 900 ms to 250 ms.\n3. Want to work on consumer banking, where a slow API is a support ticket.\n\n> I build the backend services that apps depend on, and I care a lot about how fast they feel on a phone. I'd like to hear what the mobile team is working on next.\n\n## Why Harborlight\n\n- Rebuilding their mobile backend this year: the design is still being shaped.\n- ☐ Ask how the backend team works with the iOS and Android teams.\n\n## Likely questions\n\n### How do you design an API for a mobile app?\n\n> Few round trips, small payloads, and versioning from day one, because old app versions stay in the wild for months.\n\n### Describe an outage you helped fix. ✎\n\n> A certificate expired on a Friday night. We restored service in 40 minutes, then automated renewals and added an alert 30 days out.\n\n## Questions to ask them\n\n1. How do you release backend changes without breaking older app versions?\n2. What size is the team, and who shares the pager?\n",
  "Job notes/lumenfield/outreach.md": "# Outreach: Lumenfield Software\n\n## Note to the hiring manager\n\nHi, I just applied for the Senior Software Engineer, Payments API role. I've spent the last two years owning a checkout API in Go and Postgres, and I'd love to hear how your team thinks about idempotency and retries.\n\n## Screening answers\n\n**Why this role?** You treat payments reliability as part of the product. That's how I like to work.\n\n**Earliest start date?** Two weeks after an offer.\n",
  "Job notes/lumenfield/posting.md": "# Posting: Senior Software Engineer, Payments API (Lumenfield Software)\n\nA made-up posting for the sample board.\n\n## About the role\n\nBuild and run the APIs that move money for Lumenfield's customers: payment intents, refunds and payouts, used by thousands of businesses every day.\n\n## What you'll do\n\n- Design, build and operate payment services in Go or Java.\n- Make every write safe to retry, and prove it with tests.\n- Bring p99 latency down and keep it there.\n- Mentor two mid-level engineers.\n\n## What you bring\n\n- Five or more years building backend services in production.\n- Experience with relational databases, transactions and on-call.\n",
  "Job notes/lumenfield/prep.md": "---\nasks: Design and own idempotent payment APIs | Bring p99 latency down and keep it there | Mentor two mid-level engineers\n---\n# Lumenfield Software: Senior Software Engineer, Payments API\n\n## Your opener\n\n1. Backend engineer who owns services end to end, from design doc to pager.\n2. Rebuilt the checkout API: p99 latency from 900 ms to 250 ms.\n3. Led the move from a shared database to per-service schemas for six services.\n4. Want to work closer to payments, on a team that treats reliability as a feature.\n5. Ask what a great first six months looks like to them.\n\n> I'm a backend engineer who likes owning a service end to end. Most recently I rebuilt our checkout API and cut its p99 latency from 900 to 250 milliseconds. Lumenfield's payments work looks like the next step for me.\n\n## Why Lumenfield\n\n- Their engineering blog describes payments as a product with its own roadmap: that's the work you want.\n- Small teams that own their services, including on-call.\n- ☐ Find out whether the team uses Go or Java for new services (the posting lists both).\n\n## Likely questions\n\n### Walk me through a system you designed.\n\n> The checkout API: a stateless Go service in front of Postgres, with an idempotency key on every write so retries never charge twice. I wrote the design doc, got two rounds of review, and we shipped it in six weeks.\n\n### How do you make a payment API safe to retry? ✎\n\n> Every request carries an idempotency key. The first call stores the key with the result in the same transaction; a retry with the same key returns the stored result instead of running again.\n\n- *<Keys expire after 24 hours; clients get a clear error if they reuse a key with a different body.>*\n- *<Mention the outbox pattern for events that must not be lost.>*\n\n### How do you handle on-call?\n\n> I treat alerts like bugs: every page gets a short write-up and a fix for the cause, not just the symptom. On my team we cut pages by half in two quarters by fixing the top three causes each month.\n\n### Tell me about a code review disagreement.\n\n> A teammate wanted to add a cache in front of a slow query. I suggested fixing the query first; we agreed to measure both. The index fix got us most of the way, so we skipped the cache and avoided a new failure mode.\n\n## Gaps they may probe\n\n- **No Java in production.** Bridge: you've written Go and Kotlin; the concurrency and testing habits carry over, and you've read their open-source Java client.\n- **Never led a whole team.** Bridge: you've led projects across three engineers and mentor two juniors today.\n\n## Don't say\n\n- Complaints about past managers or teammates.\n- A pay number before they share the range.\n\n## Pay {.pay}\n\n- Posted range: in the posting.\n- Your target and your floor: ☐ decide before the call (see the cheat sheet).\n\n## Questions to ask them\n\n1. Which parts of the payment flow does the team own today, and which belong to other teams?\n2. How do you decide what goes on the roadmap versus the reliability backlog?\n3. What would make this hire a clear success after a year?\n\n## What you sent them\n\n- Résumé v3 (backend version) and a cover letter that leads with the checkout API story.\n- Referral from Hana Sato on the careers site.\n",
  "Library/1_Interviews/01_Cheat_sheet.md": "# Cheat sheet (start here)\n\nRead this in the five minutes before a call. This sample also shows the markup every guide can use. Open it in a text editor next to the app to see how each piece is written.\n\n## How guides work\n\n- `## A heading` starts a card. `### A heading` starts a question inside the card. The page builds the table of contents and the jump keys from them.\n- Lines that start with `>` are what you'd say out loud. Each one gets a **Copy answer** button.\n- *<An example answer in angle brackets and stars, like this one.>* Examples show in a different color and are left out when you copy a card.\n- ✎ after a question means the answer isn't confirmed yet. The Library adds a **My version** box under it; what you type is saved to `Library/My_answers.md`.\n- ☐ marks a fact you still need to find out.\n- `::: fold Title` … `:::` makes a block you can open and close. `::: fold + Title` starts open.\n- `{.pay}` at the end of a heading marks a pay section. If you turn on `blurPay` in the settings, it stays blurred until you press H (handy when sharing your screen).\n\n::: fold Show me a folded block\nFolded blocks keep long material out of the way: a full story, a table, a posting.\n:::\n\n## Your opener\n\n1. What you build, in one line: the kind of systems and the stack.\n2. One result with a number: *<cut checkout API p99 latency from 900 ms to 250 ms>*.\n3. What you want next, and why this team fits.\n4. A question back to them.\n5. Stop there. Under a minute.\n\n> I'm a backend engineer who likes owning a service end to end, from the design doc to the pager. Most recently I rebuilt our checkout API and cut its p99 latency by more than two thirds. I'm looking for a team where reliability is part of the product, and this role reads that way.\n\n## Always asked\n\n### Why are you looking? ✎\n\n> I've shipped the big project I joined for, and I want a bigger system and more ownership of its design.\n\n- *<I want to work closer to payments and money movement.>*\n- *<My team is moving to maintenance mode and I want to keep building.>*\n\n### What's your biggest weakness? ✎\n\n> I used to go quiet when I was stuck on a hard bug. Now I timebox it: after an hour I write down what I've tried and ask for a second pair of eyes, which usually saves the day.\n\n- *<Over-polishing code before the first review; I now open draft pull requests early.>*\n- ☐ Pick a real example from the last two years.\n\n### Tell me about a failure.\n\n> I shipped a schema migration that locked a busy table for four minutes. Now every migration I write runs against a production-sized copy first, and I added that check to our pipeline.\n\n## Pay {.pay}\n\n- Your target range: ☐ look up the level on public salary sites before the recruiter call.\n- Your floor: keep it to yourself.\n- If they ask first: *<I'd like to learn more about the level first. What range did you have in mind?>*\n\n## Working with recruiters and agencies\n\n- Register with each agency once, then get to know one recruiter there who places engineers like you.\n- Only one agency should submit you for a given role. Log it on the recruiter's card under \"Roles they submitted me for\", and the Jobs tab will warn you before you apply there yourself.\n- Check in every few weeks with something useful: a new project, the stack you want next.\n\n## Never say\n\n- Anything negative about your current team or company.\n- A number before you know the level.\n",
  "Library/1_Interviews/02_Call_scripts.md": "---\nsplit: bold\n---\n# Call scripts\n\nOpening lines and questions for the calls you'll have most. `split: bold` in the front matter above lets a line in **bold** start a new question, which suits long answer lists like this one.\n\n## Recruiter screen\n\n**Open the call**\n\n> Thanks for making the time. I read the posting twice and I'd love to hear what the team is building right now.\n\n**If they ask what you're looking for** ✎\n\n> A senior backend or full-stack role on a product team, where I own services end to end and help shape the design.\n\n- *<Stack: Go or Java on the backend, Postgres, some TypeScript.>*\n- *<Remote or hybrid; open to an office two days a week.>*\n\n**Questions to ask the recruiter**\n\n1. What's the level, and who would I report to?\n2. What does the interview loop look like: coding, system design, a take-home?\n3. What size is the team, and who shares the pager?\n\n## Hiring manager\n\n**Open the call**\n\n> I've read about your platform and I have a few questions already. Could you start with what a great first six months looks like for this role?\n\n**If they ask about a weakness**\n\n> I used to go quiet when I was stuck on a hard bug. Now I timebox it and ask for a second pair of eyes after an hour, which usually saves the day.\n\n**If they ask how you pick what to work on**\n\n> I start from the user problem and the numbers: what's slow, what breaks, what costs the most support time. Then I write a one-page proposal and get feedback before I write code.\n\n## Technical rounds\n\n**Before a coding round**\n\n> Repeat the problem back, ask about input size and edge cases, say your plan out loud, then write the code and test it with one normal and one edge case.\n\n**Before a system design round**\n\n> Start with requirements and numbers, sketch the simplest design that works, then find the bottleneck and scale that part. Name the trade-offs as you go.\n\n## Agency recruiter call\n\n**Open the call**\n\n> Thanks for taking the call. I'm looking at senior backend and full-stack roles, and I'd like to understand which of your openings might fit.\n\n**What they'll ask you**\n\n- Your current stack, level and team.\n- Why now, and how quickly you can move.\n- Which companies you've already applied to, so they don't submit you twice.\n\n### If they ask about pay {.pay}\n\n> I'd rather hear the range for the role first. If it's a fit on scope, I'm confident we'll find a number that works.\n\n**Close**\n\n> What's the best way to stay in touch, and how often do you like to hear from candidates?\n",
  "Library/2_Headhunters/Recruiter_outreach.md": "# Recruiter outreach\n\nOne block per contact. A recruiter's prep sheet shows the messages for the people at that agency, next to their stage on the People tab. The format: `## Name, Agency`, then a **Connection request** and a **Follow-up message**, each as a quote.\n\n## Jonah Whitfield, Summit & Vale Partners\n\n**Connection request** (under 300 characters)\n\n> Hi Jonah, I'm a senior backend engineer (Go, Postgres, payments) starting to look for my next role. I saw you place engineers at product companies and would value a short call.\n\n**Follow-up message**\n\n> Thanks for connecting. A quick summary: I own our checkout API and cut its p99 latency by more than two thirds this year. I'm looking for a senior role on a product team. Would 15 minutes next week work?\n\n## Elena Marsh, Summit & Vale Partners\n\n**Connection request** (under 300 characters)\n\n> Hi Elena, I'm exploring senior software engineering roles and noticed you work with fintech teams. I'd be glad to be on your radar.\n\n**Follow-up message**\n\n> Thanks for connecting. If any of your openings need someone who likes owning reliable services end to end, I'd love to talk.\n"
 }
};
